te things refer back to God, and there is only one real sense of causality across the order of being.\n\nSPINOZA’s ontology is complemented by a precise epistemology centered on ideas: the mind is the idea of the body, and there is a fundamental parallelism (isomorphy, isonomie, isology) between mental and bodily processes, such that knowledge of effects implies knowledge of causes. He distinguishes adequate from inadequate ideas and outlines how cognition progresses from affections to clearer concepts, with the notion of common notions (notions communes) playing a crucial role in forming general ideas. He also emphasizes the genetics of understanding; definitions can be nominal or genetic, and the pursuit of intuitive knowledge represents a third kind of understanding beyond imagination and reasoning from experience.\n\nEthically, SPINOZA introduces conatus—the innate drive to persevere in existence—and analyzes how affections (joy, sadness, love, hatred) modulate the power to act. Joy tends to increase power and unite the self with what is loved, while sadness diminishes it. He maintains that freedom is not a property of the will, which is always determined by causes, and he ties human emplace within a deterministic order to the unity of God and Nature. The eternity of essences (species aeternitatis) and the persistence of essence are essential elements, linking ethical life to rational understanding of the divine order.\n\nIn political theory, SPINOZA treats the state as arising from a social contract in which individuals relinquish some power for the collective strength formed by shared affections of hope and fear. This absolutum imperium can take monarchic, aristocratic, or democratic forms, with democracy most closely resembling the ideal of substituting the love of liberty for passion-driven fear and hope.\n\nSPINOZA’s methodological stance is geometric in spirit, emphasizing clear definitions and demonstrations, the role of signs and interpretation in knowle